Part 3: Pico JSON format

Modify src/main.c send_state() if needed so Pico sends this JSON to POST /update:

During game:

{
  "state": "GAME_PLAYING",
  "p1": "Guest",
  "p1_uid": "DBEF7005",
  "p2": "Guest",
  "p2_uid": "4C069804",
  "score_a": 3,
  "score_b": 2,
  "fastest": 12.4,
  "winner": "",
  "winner_uid": "",
  "time": 80
}

When finished:

{
  "state": "GAME_OVER",
  "p1": "Sara",
  "p1_uid": "DBEF7005",
  "p2": "Ali",
  "p2_uid": "4C069804",
  "score_a": 5,
  "score_b": 3,
  "fastest": 12.4,
  "winner": "Sara",
  "winner_uid": "DBEF7005",
  "time": 160
}

RFID UID must be sent as uppercase hex string without spaces.

Part 4: Server endpoint POST /update

Implement POST /update.

Behavior:
- Receive JSON from Pico.
- Read p1_uid and p2_uid.
- For each UID:
  - If player exists, use existing player.
  - If player does not exist, create guest player automatically.
  - Guest names should be Guest1, Guest2, Guest3, etc.
- Update live_state table with:
  - state
  - player1_id
  - player2_id
  - score1
  - score2
  - fastest_shot
  - winner_id
  - updated_at
- If winner_uid exists, map it to winner_id.
- If state is GAME_OVER, save one match in matches table.
- Avoid saving the same finished match repeatedly, because the Pico may continue sending GAME_OVER every second.
- Use a simple duplicate protection:
  - Save a GAME_OVER match only if the last saved match does not have the same player1_id, player2_id, score1, score2, and ended_at within the last few seconds.
  - Or track previous live_state transition from non-GAME_OVER to GAME_OVER.

